WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
This lesson walks through clarity and specificity in prompt instructions.

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.159
We'll look at why vague instructions produce inconsistent outputs,

3
00:00:08.159 --> 00:00:11.920
why specificity is the primary lever for output quality,

4
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.040
how the direct instruction principle works, how ambiguity forces the model to fill gaps

5
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:23.440
with assumptions, and what imprecise prompts actually cost across a full iteration cycle.

6
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:24.319
Let's get into it.

7
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.440
Precision in an instruction isn't a stylistic preference.

8
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.919
It is the main thing separating a reliable output from a coin flip.

9
00:00:33.919 --> 00:00:38.799
Specificity is the degree to which an instruction precisely defines the expected output,

10
00:00:38.799 --> 00:00:43.840
and when it is high, the model has very little room to interpret or fill gaps on its own.

11
00:00:44.479 --> 00:00:46.959
Ambiguity is the opposite condition.

12
00:00:46.959 --> 00:00:50.560
When an instruction has more than one plausible interpretation,

13
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.880
the model has to choose between them.

14
00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.759
That choice is never visible to you, and it may not be the choice you intended.

15
00:00:57.759 --> 00:01:01.520
Direct instruction means stating what you want the model to do

16
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.439
rather than what you want it to avoid.

17
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.879
Negative framing tells the model what to skip but leaves the positive goal undefined.

18
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.040
Let's start with why vague instructions fail.

19
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:17.519
When a prompt is open-ended, the model doesn't stall or ask for clarification.

20
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.559
It resolves the openness by selecting the most statistically likely interpretation

21
00:01:22.559 --> 00:01:24.559
and produces output from that choice.

22
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.400
Each plausible interpretation produces a different output,

23
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.480
so run the same vague prompt twice and you get two different results.

24
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:34.959
That variance isn't a model defect,

25
00:01:34.959 --> 00:01:39.919
it's a direct reflection of how much room the prompt leaves for interpretation.

26
00:01:39.919 --> 00:01:42.559
And the reading it lands on may not be yours,

27
00:01:42.559 --> 00:01:45.519
while neither party is aware another one was even possible.

28
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.080
The output looks plausible because it is plausible, it just isn't what you wanted.

29
00:01:50.639 --> 00:01:54.480
Precision at the prompt stage prevents that entire class of problems.

30
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.599
Let's make the difference between abstract and concrete instructions

31
00:01:59.599 --> 00:02:01.360
visible with a direct comparison.

32
00:02:01.919 --> 00:02:05.040
An abstract vague instruction might read like this.

33
00:02:05.599 --> 00:02:07.360
Write a summary of this document.

34
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:11.679
That prompt forces the model to decide length, format, tone,

35
00:02:11.679 --> 00:02:16.240
level of detail, intended audience, and what counts as the key points.

36
00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.320
Every one of those decisions is an assumption and none of them are visible to you.

37
00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.119
A concrete, specific version of the same instruction reads differently.

38
00:02:25.119 --> 00:02:29.600
Write a three-sentence summary of this document for a non-technical audience,

39
00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:34.320
including the main finding, one supporting detail, and the recommended action.

40
00:02:35.039 --> 00:02:40.320
The second version closes all six of those interpretation gaps in a single sentence.

41
00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:43.919
The output becomes predictable because the decision space is closed.

42
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.759
Specificity is the single most reliable variable under your control when writing instructions.

43
00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.479
Every added dimension of specificity narrows the output space further.

44
00:02:55.119 --> 00:02:58.399
Specifying format removes ambiguity about structure.

45
00:02:59.039 --> 00:03:02.160
Specifying length removes ambiguity about scope.

46
00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:06.240
Specifying tone and perspective removes ambiguity about voice.

47
00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.960
Each constraint compounds on the others.

48
00:03:09.839 --> 00:03:15.360
A prompt with four specific constraints produces a much narrower output space

49
00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.639
than a prompt with one, and that narrower space is where consistent, repeatable outputs live.

50
00:03:21.199 --> 00:03:24.800
When an output disappoints, the right question is almost always,

51
00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.039
what did I leave unspecified that the model had to assume?

52
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:34.720
Identifying that gap and closing it is usually the fastest path to the output you actually want.

53
00:03:35.039 --> 00:03:39.199
The direct instruction principle says to state what you want the model to do,

54
00:03:39.759 --> 00:03:41.279
not what you want it to avoid.

55
00:03:41.919 --> 00:03:45.440
Negative framing, like don't be vague or avoid jargon,

56
00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:50.240
tells the model what to subtract, but leaves the positive goal undefined.

57
00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.600
The model still has to guess what the output should look like.

58
00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.919
Direct instructions, by contrast, map to concrete actions.

59
00:03:57.919 --> 00:04:02.960
Write, summarize, list, compare, extract, classify.

60
00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.839
Each of those verbs specifies a clear operation.

61
00:04:07.839 --> 00:04:11.039
Keeping one goal per instruction also matters.

62
00:04:11.039 --> 00:04:15.839
Compound instructions that combine multiple objectives split the model's focus

63
00:04:15.839 --> 00:04:18.880
and reduce the reliability of each individual goal.

64
00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.880
State one goal, then layer constraints on top of it.

65
00:04:24.079 --> 00:04:27.279
Let's talk about the actual cost of ambiguous prompts,

66
00:04:27.279 --> 00:04:30.320
because it shows up in places that are easy to underestimate.

67
00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:35.119
Variance in outputs means you can't build reliable expectations

68
00:04:35.119 --> 00:04:36.799
about what a prompt will return.

69
00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.959
Unpredictability makes quality evaluation harder,

70
00:04:40.959 --> 00:04:46.000
because you have to account for the fact that the same prompt might perform differently tomorrow.

71
00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.839
Debugging ambiguous outputs is expensive.

72
00:04:49.839 --> 00:04:54.079
You have to go back to the prompt, identify what gap the model filled,

73
00:04:54.079 --> 00:04:57.119
decide what you actually wanted, and rewrite.

74
00:04:57.839 --> 00:05:02.000
Do that across dozens of iterations, and the time adds up fast.

75
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.880
The cost of ambiguity is hidden at writing time,

76
00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.720
and fully paid at review time, which is the worst possible accounting.

77
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:14.480
There are four reliable ways to turn a vague instruction into a specific one.

78
00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.839
Add a format, tell the model whether you want a bullet list,

79
00:05:17.839 --> 00:05:21.040
numbered steps, a table, or a paragraph.

80
00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:25.440
That single constraint eliminates a major category of interpretation gaps.

81
00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:31.679
Add a length, specify word count, sentence count, or a natural bound, like in one paragraph.

82
00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:37.119
Add an audience, naming who will read the output determines tone,

83
00:05:37.119 --> 00:05:40.640
vocabulary level, and assumed background knowledge.

84
00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:46.559
Add a perspective, first person, third person, or a named role, like senior analyst.

85
00:05:46.559 --> 00:05:50.559
Each of these dimensions is a constraint you can apply independently,

86
00:05:50.559 --> 00:05:53.519
and any one of them meaningfully narrows the output space.

87
00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:58.640
A model getting something wrong and a model resolving a choice you never made

88
00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:02.640
are not the same failure. Ambiguity is that second case.

89
00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:07.359
Every gap in an instruction gets filled with an assumption silently,

90
00:06:07.359 --> 00:06:10.880
and the output looks plausible because it is plausible.

91
00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:15.839
Once you see it that way, adding another constraint stops feeling like fussiness,

92
00:06:15.839 --> 00:06:19.119
and starts looking like the cheapest debugging you'll ever do.

93
00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:23.760
That's where we'll end this lesson. I'll see you in the next one. Bye.

